---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Shot from '../components/Shot.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';

const B = '/work/alucify';
const FIGMA =
  'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="A tool for reviewing code an AI agent wrote"
  metaTitle="Alucify — designing for AI agents working on a codebase | Tyler Young"
  description="A code-intelligence tool whose primary metrics are blast radius, agent hours and token cost — designed for people accountable for work an agent did."
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Product"
  meta={[
    ['Product', 'Code intelligence — “your code, flows, and intents, unified”'],
    ['Contribution', 'All product design — every surface, every state'],
    ['Surface', 'First run through tenured use · 20 documented states'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      An AI agent writes the code. Someone still has to sign off on it. They did
      not watch it get written, so they need to know what it touched, how much work
      it was, and what it cost. I designed the tool that tells them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/dashboard.png`}
    alt="Alucify dashboard for a project called RBAC v1.1.1, showing artifact status of one integrated, seven critical issues and twelve major issues; coverage analysis at 89 percent overall with three partially covered and one not covered; and impact analysis showing 87 percent blast radius, 240 agent hours and a 5M token cost."
    state="Tenured user dashboard"
    caption="Blast radius, agent hours and token cost sit at the same level as coverage. Customer name redacted in the rail."
    wide
  />

  <h2>The three numbers on the landing screen</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Blast radius, agent hours, token cost. Not velocity, not lines written. You
      see how far the change reached, how much work it was and what it cost before
      you open anything.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Cost shows up again on each individual change, next to what it paid for. Most
      tools bury it in a billing page, where it never affects a decision.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage, broken into decisions</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Under the percentage is every decision behind it. Each one is named,
      categorised as scope, architecture or data, and marked covered, partial or
      none. Worst first, so the gaps are the first thing you see.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/coverage.png`}
    alt="Coverage analysis view showing 89 percent overall across 27 total decisions, with 23 fully covered, 3 partially covered and 1 not covered, above a table of named AI decisions each tagged none, partial or covered."
    state="Coverage analysis"
    caption="Every row is a specific decision — a gateway type resolution spec, a cache hydration model — not an abstract category."
    wide
  />

  <h2>How severity is shown</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      This is the one place the product raises its voice. A change marked critical
      gets a summary and a breakdown across blast radius, regression risk, data
      integrity and backward compatibility. Everything else stays quiet so this
      reads as urgent.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Shot
    src={`${B}/impact.png`}
    alt="Impact analysis detail panel for a scoped change boundary marked critical, two of five, with a written summary and severity indicators for blast radius at 30 percent, 56 agent hours, 3M token cost, regression, data integrity and backward compatibility."
    state="Impact analysis — concept detail"
    caption="Severity is carried by a badge, a colour and a written summary together, not by colour alone."
    wide
  />

  <h2>Empty, loading and stale states</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      I designed twenty screens. Many are the ones usually left to engineering to
      improvise: an integration still running, an audit gone stale, a dashboard
      with nothing in it yet. People spend most of their time in these states, not
      in the full one.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="pair">
    <Shot
      src={`${B}/reaudit.png`}
      alt="Artifact re-audit view with a full-width orange banner stating the artifact was integrated on a given date, a deprecation notice reading this audit has been deprecated, please re-audit, and a table of six issues tagged critical, major and minor, each paired with a written resolution."
      state="Deprecated audit"
      caption="A stale result is stated plainly and paired with the action that fixes it."
    />
    <Shot
      src={`${B}/in-progress.png`}
      alt="Artifact integration in progress, showing the dashboard skeleton with empty metric slots and a yellow status banner explaining that context is being created and a completion notification will follow by email."
      state="Integration in progress"
      caption="The empty state explains what is happening and what will end the wait."
    />
  </div>

  <h2>All twenty screens</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Four flows. Getting set up runs from signing in to a context the product can
      reason about. The other three all start on the same dashboard, because that
      is the hub — artifacts, coverage and impact are the three ways out of it.
      Open the file to zoom into any of them.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <a class="canvas" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    <img
      src={`${B}/figma-canvas.jpg`}
      alt="The Alucify design file: twenty screens laid out in four rows — eight for getting set up, six for artifacts, three for coverage and three for impact."
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      width="2662"
      height="1070"
    />
    <span class="canvas-bar">
      <span class="canvas-label">Open the design file</span>
      <span class="canvas-meta">20 frames · 4 flows · Figma <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
    </span>
  </a>

  <h2>What I owned</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      All of the design. Every screen and every state — the metrics on the landing
      screen, the coverage model, the severity system, and the twenty states from
      first run to daily use.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Shown with the founder's agreement. The customer name has been redacted from
      the navigation rail in every screen; the product and its data model are
      otherwise as designed. The values on these screens are sample data from the
      design file — they exercise the layout, they are not a customer's
      measurements.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  /* The whole canvas is the target. It is deliberately not legible at this size —
     it shows the shape and extent of the work, and the click is what makes it
     readable. Anything more detailed here would compete with the file itself. */
  .canvas {
    display: block;
    margin: 0 0 34px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: #1e1e1e;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .canvas img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .canvas-bar {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 8px 18px;
    padding: 13px 16px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .canvas-label {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-ink);
    padding-bottom: 2px;
  }

  .canvas-meta {
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .canvas:hover .canvas-label {
    color: var(--color-signal);
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-signal);
  }

  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 36px !important;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: 20px;
    margin-bottom: 34px;
  }

  .pair :global(.shot) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
